(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-11"] = {"4.1":{"s":"A definite integral of a continuous function over [a, b] can be defined as the limit of a sum: split the interval into n equal strips, add f at each strip's point times its width, and let n grow without limit. For a positive function this is the area under the curve between x = a and x = b.","w":["With h = (b − a)/n, the sum uses f(a + rh) for r = 1 to n; let n → ∞ (h → 0) only at the end."]},"4.2":{"s":"Fundamental theorem: if g is any antiderivative of a continuous f, then the integral from a to b equals g(b) − g(a). The constant c cancels, so a definite integral is a number, not a function. a is the lower limit and b the upper limit.","f":[{"x":"∫_a^b f(x) dx = g(b) - g(a)","n":"Where g is an antiderivative of f (g' = f).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.157"}],"w":["No + c in a definite integral; it cancels."]},"4.3":{"s":"Properties make definite integrals easier. Swapping the limits changes the sign. The variable's name does not matter. The interval can be split at any point. Replacing x by a + b − x leaves the integral unchanged, which often lets two forms be added to cancel a hard part. Over a symmetric interval [−a, a], an odd function integrates to zero and an even one to twice the integral over [0, a].","f":[{"x":"∫_a^b f(x) dx = -∫_b^a f(x) dx","n":"Swapping the limits changes the sign.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.157"},{"x":"∫_a^b f(x) dx = ∫_a^c f(x) dx + ∫_c^b f(x) dx","n":"Splitting at c, a < c < b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.158"},{"x":"∫_a^b f(x) dx = ∫_a^b f(a + b - x) dx","n":"King's property (Property V); for limits 0 to a it becomes the integral of f(x) = the integral of f(a - x) (Property VI, p.160).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.159"},{"x":"∫_0^(2a) f(x) dx = ∫_0^a f(x) dx + ∫_0^a f(2a - x) dx","n":"Property VII.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.161"},{"x":"∫_(-a)^a f(x) dx = 2*∫_0^a f(x) dx","n":"If f is even (f(-x) = f(x)); the integral is 0 if f is odd.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.161"}],"w":["Check odd/even first: an odd integrand over [−a, a] gives 0 straight away.","For f(x)/(f(x) + f(a + b − x)) type integrals, add I to its reflected form: 2I = ∫1 dx."]}};
